Objective
Design (and optionally implement) an FSM-controlled LED engine that updates LED patterns only on a timed tick, reinforcing the separation between time generation and control logic.

What You’re Building
A synchronous LED pattern engine with multiple operating modes, selected and controlled by a finite state machine.
Modes (FSM states):
1. Wrap chaser – LED shifts in one direction and wraps around.
2. Ping-pong – LED bounces left ↔ right using a direction bit.
3. Binary counter – LEDs count upward as an 8-bit value.

Core Design Rules
All logic is clocked and synchronous.
Time comes from a counter/tick, not from loops or delays.
LED state updates only on a tick.
FSM state transitions occur only on a tick.
One clock domain, one clocked process (or clean 2-process FSM if preferred).

Signals to Define
tick — 1-cycle enable from a counter
led_reg(7 downto 0) — registered LED state
mode(1 downto 0) — FSM state
dir — direction bit for ping-pong mode
mode_cnt — counts ticks to auto-advance modes (temporary stand-in for a button)

Reset Behavior
LEDs start at "10000000"
FSM starts in wrap chaser mode
Direction resets to a known value
All counters reset cleanly

Expected Outcome
By the end of the session you should be able to:
Explain how FSMs sit on top of timing logic
Describe LED behavior in terms of state + tick
Confidently sketch or code the FSM without guessing
Be ready to later swap mode_cnt for a debounced button event